High in the cold north, Greyhollow is a town of soot-stained stone and timber, its houses and smithies cut into the flank of the mountain where the silver and iron come out. The dwarves there keep the old faith of the Anvil Father, who watches the forge, the stone, and the dead beneath it. Brakka Ironvein has stood guard at His temple for six years, mail on his back and the smell of incense and coal in his clothes.

Three days ago three miners went down into the old north workings, which had been sealed a generation back, and did not come up. Last night a fourth came out of the dark alone, raving about lights that walk, and died before morning. The town council has had enough: at sunrise tomorrow they will brick the mouth shut, whether the missing men are out or not. Hessa, the temple's old prior, would not leave them to that. She asked Brakka to go down before dawn and bring back whoever can still be brought back.

Now it is late afternoon. Brakka stands at the mine mouth, the temple's hooded lantern in his hand. The old timbers sag; the seal is broken open, and the passage drops away into cold, wet darkness. The last sun is on the peaks, but nothing warmer reaches the opening.

What do you do?
